The monthly refresh gates on npm test, not the browser suite, so an e2e pin on a converted figure broke
after the first real FX refresh. docs/DATA_FRESHNESS.md 'Pinned values' and the playbook say so.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014jaqot5Ddmi7Co6CofBiyc
---
 docs/DATA_FRESHNESS.md   |  5 +++++
 docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md |  4 ++++
 test/e2e/fix-app.e2e.js  | 10 +++++++---
 3 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/DATA_FRESHNESS.md b/docs/DATA_FRESHNESS.md
index 3287a8a..e481d34 100644
--- a/docs/DATA_FRESHNESS.md
+++ b/docs/DATA_FRESHNESS.md
@@ -312,6 +312,11 @@ Some tests and docs pin numbers that move when the data refreshes:
 
 `scripts/refresh/derived.js` regenerates the generated blocks (`<!-- freshness:... -->` markers) in each refresh PR. Hand-written figures are checked and reported as drift.
 
+The refresh's gate runs `npm test`, not the browser suite (`scripts/e2e.js`). So an e2e test that pins a converted
+figure passes the gate and breaks after a refresh. The first real FX refresh (2026-10-16) did this to Fix app
+[P-5] ("£80,000 ≈ $108K" became $107K). E2e tests build their expected figures from the app's own rates (`toUSD`
+from public/viz/palette.js), never from a literal.
+
 Intentional golden vectors, such as the tax tests, change only together with a verified rule change. The list of pins and their owners is in [process/freshness-derived.md](process/freshness-derived.md) and [process/freshness-audit-surface.md](process/freshness-audit-surface.md).
 
 ### History and the archive
diff --git a/docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md b/docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md
index 433b44d..07fe376 100644
--- a/docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md
+++ b/docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md
@@ -172,6 +172,10 @@ don't lose that much." So:
 
 ## Known traps
 
+- **E2e tests that pin converted pay break after an FX refresh.** The monthly refresh commits once `npm test`
+  passes; it does not run the browser suite. Fix app [P-5] pinned "£80,000 ≈ $108K", and the September rates made it
+  $107K. In e2e tests, build expected figures from `toUSD` (public/viz/palette.js), as the test's own `want` value
+  already did, never from a literal (docs/DATA_FRESHNESS.md "Pinned values").
 - **Phones come in more sizes than 360, 390 and 430.** On 2026-10-16 the phone matrix (test/e2e/phone-sizes.mjs,
   run by test/e2e/webkit/phone-smoke.mjs and webkit-phone.yml) found the Razr cover screen (352x355) broken: the
   landscape rule `(max-width: 860px) and (max-height: 500px)` caught a square screen, the top bar laid out at
diff --git a/test/e2e/fix-app.e2e.js b/test/e2e/fix-app.e2e.js
index d8feca0..ca5004d 100644
--- a/test/e2e/fix-app.e2e.js
+++ b/test/e2e/fix-app.e2e.js
@@ -228,11 +228,15 @@ export function registerFixAppTests(suite) {
     const box = filterSec(page, 'Salary').locator('input.range-val').first();
     assertEq((await filterSec(page, 'Salary').locator('.range-vals > label > span').first().innerText()).trim(), 'Min $/yr', 'Min label');
     const k = (n) => Math.round(n / 1000) * 1000;
+    // The converted figure follows the current FX rates (the monthly refresh changes them; 2026-10-16 moved
+    // £80,000 from $108K to $107K), so the expected text is built from the same rate the app uses.
+    const shown = (usd) => `\\$${usd / 1000}K`;
+    const gbp80 = k(toUSD(80000, 'GBP')), gbp250 = k(toUSD(250000, 'GBP')), aud350 = k(toUSD(350000, 'AUD'));
     const cases = [
-      ['£80,000', k(toUSD(80000, 'GBP')), /£80,000 ≈ \$108K a year/],
-      ['£250,000', k(toUSD(250000, 'GBP')), /£250,000 ≈ \$337K/],
+      ['£80,000', gbp80, new RegExp(`£80,000 ≈ ${shown(gbp80)} a year`)],
+      ['£250,000', gbp250, new RegExp(`£250,000 ≈ ${shown(gbp250)}`)],
       ['80k', 80000, /below the lowest pay listed here/],
-      ['A$350k', k(toUSD(350000, 'AUD')), /A\$350,000 ≈ \$245K/],
+      ['A$350k', aud350, new RegExp(`A\\$350,000 ≈ ${shown(aud350)}`)],
       ['AUD 350000', k(toUSD(350000, 'AUD')), /A\$350,000 ≈/],
       ['60000', 60000, /below the lowest pay/],
       ['400', 400000, /“400” read as \$400K a year/],
